1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,700
Hi, students. In this lecture

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,560
of advanced embedded
software development,

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,710
we're going to be talking about
system programming and do

4
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,270
our first discussion
of what we mean when

5
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:10,920
we talk about
system programming,

6
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:12,330
which is what we're going
to focus on for the

7
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:14,745
first third or so
of this course.

8
00:00:14,745 --> 00:00:16,995
Learning objectives for this,

9
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:20,550
for today will be to
introduce system programming,

10
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:22,890
understand APIs and ABIs

11
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:24,225
and the difference
between the two,

12
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:27,390
and also to introduce
the POSIX standard.

13
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:30,150
What is system software?

14
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:31,880
The book defines this as

15
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:36,210
interfacing between the
kernel and the C library.

16
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:38,990
Common examples of system

17
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:40,220
programming that you could find.

18
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,140
It could be an GUI, it
could be a compiler,

19
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:44,570
debugger, web
server or database.

20
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:45,980
Really, there's a variety of

21
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:49,175
software that could be
considered system software.

22
00:00:49,175 --> 00:00:51,140
The book draws a
distinction between

23
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:53,390
system software and
application software.

24
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,640
In that application
software might

25
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:57,770
use higher-level libraries,

26
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:01,825
whereas what we're calling
system software does not.

27
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:04,025
With system software, there's

28
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:07,355
less details about the OS
and hardware abstracted.

29
00:01:07,355 --> 00:01:09,800
The reason we're going to
focus on system software for

30
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,800
this course is because we
want to get into the details.

31
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,000
We're going to be working at

32
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,480
the OS and kernel layer
and we're going to

33
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,850
use that for the second
third of this course where

34
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:23,165
we start talking about
programming in kernel land.

35
00:01:23,165 --> 00:01:25,220
Some of the cornerstones of

36
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:27,770
system programming and how
you do system programming.

37
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:29,480
One is system calls,

38
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:31,310
also known as syscalls,

39
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:33,080
which is how you
get into and out of

40
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,075
the kernel for anything

41
00:01:35,075 --> 00:01:37,025
that deals with interaction
with the kernel.

42
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:39,960
A second is the C library.

43
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:43,385
Commonly used one is libc.

44
00:01:43,385 --> 00:01:46,460
Then the third cornerstone is
the C compiler and linker,

45
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,715
which is a part of a toolchain.

46
00:01:48,715 --> 00:01:51,950
Syscalls or system calls are

47
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:54,330
kernel function invocations
from user space.

48
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:55,790
This is something you can use

49
00:01:55,790 --> 00:01:57,380
to have the kernel do some piece

50
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,870
of work for you from
your application.

51
00:02:00,870 --> 00:02:02,630
Read and write are examples

52
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:04,895
of the type of things that would

53
00:02:04,895 --> 00:02:07,880
be a system call where
the kernel would be doing

54
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:10,640
some work for your user
space application.

55
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,120
There's roughly 300 of
these system calls in total

56
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,340
and around 90
percent of those are

57
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,565
implemented by all
architectures.

58
00:02:18,565 --> 00:02:22,220
In other words, the
system call interface is

59
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:24,995
pretty common across different

60
00:02:24,995 --> 00:02:27,155
hardware architectures, that is.

61
00:02:27,155 --> 00:02:29,420
The second cornerstone is

62
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:32,795
the C Library or in
the case of GNU,

63
00:02:32,795 --> 00:02:36,040
it'd be libc or glibc.

64
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,510
This is the wrapper
for the system call,

65
00:02:38,510 --> 00:02:40,880
which gives you the ability to

66
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:43,645
call from your C application.

67
00:02:43,645 --> 00:02:45,395
The header file
that you include,

68
00:02:45,395 --> 00:02:46,850
there's a function
prototype that you

69
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:49,400
call and underneath that
function prototype,

70
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,245
they'll ultimately be a call to

71
00:02:51,245 --> 00:02:54,895
the syscall interface
into the kernel.

72
00:02:54,895 --> 00:02:57,000
The third cornerstone, again,

73
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,385
is the C compiler and
linker in the toolchain.

74
00:02:59,385 --> 00:03:01,550
We're going to focus on GCC and

75
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:04,770
the GNU C compiler
for this course.

76
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:07,130
One of the things that the book

77
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:08,840
mentions is that
it's not possible to

78
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:10,700
link your users pace application

79
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:12,620
directly with the Linux kernel.

80
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:15,860
That's a design
decision in terms

81
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:19,375
of how the Linux operating
system was designed.

82
00:03:19,375 --> 00:03:22,369
For security and
reliability reasons,

83
00:03:22,369 --> 00:03:24,620
it's better not to
have a direct link

84
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,395
between your user space
application and the kernel.

85
00:03:27,395 --> 00:03:30,035
Instead, the way we get

86
00:03:30,035 --> 00:03:31,970
functions performed
in the kernel space

87
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:33,740
is to use what's called a trap.

88
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:36,780
In a lot of cases, this
is a software interrupt.

89
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:39,290
If you look at a simple
Hello World application

90
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:40,750
that's doing a printf,

91
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:42,560
that printf is
going to be calling

92
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,930
into the POSIX API library,

93
00:03:44,930 --> 00:03:47,180
and then the POSIX
API library is going

94
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:49,220
to be doing a trap
that would invoke

95
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,320
a kernel space function that
would do something like

96
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:54,870
print to the terminal
in the case of printf.

97
00:03:55,250 --> 00:03:59,205
How does the POSIX
interface work?

98
00:03:59,205 --> 00:04:01,860
How is the interface
between user space

99
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:04,125
and the POSIX library defined?

100
00:04:04,125 --> 00:04:05,260
This is part of what's called

101
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,210
an Application
Programming Interface.

102
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:09,700
An API is really just
a definition about

103
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:10,960
how one piece of

104
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,265
software can talk to
another piece of software.

105
00:04:13,265 --> 00:04:15,850
The software that
provides the API is

106
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:19,060
the implementation of
the API definition.

107
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:21,849
The API is used to ensure
source compatibility,

108
00:04:21,849 --> 00:04:24,190
meaning that you can
write the source once,

109
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:25,810
you can compile it for x86,

110
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:27,330
you can compile it for ARM.

111
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:29,220
Do any different architecture.

112
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:32,725
As long as the source
matches the AI definition,

113
00:04:32,725 --> 00:04:34,300
it's going to be able
to be recompiled

114
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,100
and rerun on
different platforms.

115
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,950
Your source code
can remain portable

116
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:43,145
across hardware
platforms and revisions.

117
00:04:43,145 --> 00:04:45,840
Ideally, there are some
limitations on this.

118
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,500
We mentioned before
that syscall has

119
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,795
about 90 percent
architecture similarities,

120
00:04:51,795 --> 00:04:54,080
so that means that there's
approximately 10 percent

121
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:56,420
of syscalls that
might be architecture

122
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:58,220
independent and there might

123
00:04:58,220 --> 00:04:59,570
be some functions that you could

124
00:04:59,570 --> 00:05:03,220
call via an API that would
be architecture specific.

125
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:04,810
But for the most part,

126
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:06,200
the APIs are going to be

127
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:07,970
widely cross-platform

128
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:09,950
and used across
different architectures.

129
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:13,625
For any of the common C
library functions like printf,

130
00:05:13,625 --> 00:05:15,380
string copy, etc,

131
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:17,090
you're going to
be able to easily

132
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:18,920
recompile for a different
hardware platform

133
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:20,660
and run without issue

134
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:23,730
after you complete
the compiling like.

135
00:05:24,510 --> 00:05:27,760
To compare with the API,

136
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,095
there's also what's called the

137
00:05:29,095 --> 00:05:32,785
ABI or the application
binary interface.

138
00:05:32,785 --> 00:05:35,950
Before we were talking
about source compatibility.

139
00:05:35,950 --> 00:05:39,100
The ABI is talking about
binary compatibility.

140
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:41,020
It's saying, after you
do your compile and

141
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,705
your link step and you have
an executable program,

142
00:05:43,705 --> 00:05:46,120
where is that program
going to be able to run?

143
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:47,710
That's what's ultimately defined

144
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:49,810
by the application
binary interface.

145
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:51,280
Some of the things
that's covered by

146
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:53,020
the application binary interface

147
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,625
would be calling conventions,

148
00:05:54,625 --> 00:05:56,530
byte ordering or register use.

149
00:05:56,530 --> 00:05:59,004
Meaning if you called
some function,

150
00:05:59,004 --> 00:06:01,240
there would be some convention

151
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:03,850
about where the first
argument would go,

152
00:06:03,850 --> 00:06:06,355
in which register of a
specific architecture.

153
00:06:06,355 --> 00:06:07,795
It's easy to see that,

154
00:06:07,795 --> 00:06:10,780
that wouldn't be portable
across different architectures.

155
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,760
For instance, x86 doesn't

156
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:15,220
have the same register
expect that ARM would.

157
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:17,350
It makes sense that
there would have to be

158
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:18,460
a different ABI between

159
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:21,230
those two different
hardware architectures.

160
00:06:21,450 --> 00:06:24,460
The ABI is defined and

161
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:27,805
implemented by the kernel
and the toolchain.

162
00:06:27,805 --> 00:06:35,250
The ABI defines the program's
interaction with itself,

163
00:06:35,250 --> 00:06:37,560
with libraries, and
with the kernel.

164
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:39,930
With itself, what I mean is,

165
00:06:39,930 --> 00:06:42,330
when you call a function
in your own program,

166
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:44,880
that's going to use an ABI
to decide for instance,

167
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:47,080
where to put the parameters

168
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,310
that are passed
into the function.

169
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:53,050
The ultimate lead ABIs

170
00:06:53,050 --> 00:06:54,460
going to used to
generate that binary

171
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:59,215
that's the output of the
compiler and link step.

172
00:06:59,215 --> 00:07:02,200
In order to know if your binary

173
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:04,930
is going to run on a
different platform,

174
00:07:04,930 --> 00:07:07,480
what you have to know
is how close of a match

175
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,370
do you have between

176
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:11,290
the target that you
originally compiled

177
00:07:11,290 --> 00:07:13,465
for and the target that
you're trying to run on.

178
00:07:13,465 --> 00:07:15,475
Some of the things are
going to impact whether

179
00:07:15,475 --> 00:07:18,310
an ABI is portable to a
different platform would

180
00:07:18,310 --> 00:07:20,860
be obviously the hardware
architecture like I just

181
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:23,980
mentioned between ARM
and x86 for instance,

182
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:27,160
likely those would not
share a similar ABI.

183
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:30,145
But there would also
be software libraries.

184
00:07:30,145 --> 00:07:32,290
Whether you use glibc,

185
00:07:32,290 --> 00:07:35,320
and also which version of
glibc used would be important.

186
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:36,880
The compiler would be important,

187
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:39,160
because that's part of
the definition of how,

188
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:44,150
for instance, the parameters
will be passed in registers.

189
00:07:45,750 --> 00:07:51,920
We talked about API definitions
and API is being defined.

190
00:07:53,750 --> 00:07:56,640
One implementation of
API definition for

191
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:58,845
user space is in
what's called POSIX,

192
00:07:58,845 --> 00:08:01,925
which stands for portable
operating system interface.

193
00:08:01,925 --> 00:08:03,700
It was started back in

194
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,950
the late 1980s as a way to

195
00:08:05,950 --> 00:08:08,755
coalesce what the book
calls the Unix Wars.

196
00:08:08,755 --> 00:08:11,560
If you look at more history

197
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:14,140
of the Unix and Linux
operating system,

198
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:16,600
what you see is a
chart like this.

199
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:19,885
In the late '70s and early '80s,

200
00:08:19,885 --> 00:08:21,670
there were several
different branches that

201
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:23,935
developed where different
versions of Unix

202
00:08:23,935 --> 00:08:28,120
originally and ultimately
Linux were started and each

203
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:29,260
one of them were doing

204
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:32,470
slightly different things
in terms of API calls.

205
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:35,710
There was a need, especially
in the late '80s,

206
00:08:35,710 --> 00:08:37,600
as all these numbers of

207
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,435
different Unix and Linux
variants started to show up,

208
00:08:40,435 --> 00:08:43,870
there was a strong desire
for programmers to coalesce

209
00:08:43,870 --> 00:08:46,540
how the API works and
make sure that when you

210
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:49,945
write software for one platform,

211
00:08:49,945 --> 00:08:51,760
you'll be able to
recompile it and run it on

212
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:53,500
a different platform
without having to

213
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:56,035
do custom API work
for a platform.

214
00:08:56,035 --> 00:08:57,640
That was the original motivation

215
00:08:57,640 --> 00:09:00,260
behind the POSIX platform.

216
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:05,170
The Linux operating system

217
00:09:05,170 --> 00:09:07,675
that we're going to use as
the subject of this course.

218
00:09:07,675 --> 00:09:08,950
You can actually
go back here and

219
00:09:08,950 --> 00:09:10,540
click on this Google
Groups link and you can

220
00:09:10,540 --> 00:09:12,310
see the original posting to

221
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:15,520
the Minix mailing list

222
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:19,630
where Linus Torvalds proposed
the Linux operating system,

223
00:09:19,630 --> 00:09:21,685
and this would
have been in 1991.

224
00:09:21,685 --> 00:09:23,440
This was around the time that

225
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,630
the POSIX standard was
starting to be defined and

226
00:09:25,630 --> 00:09:29,050
everybody was working towards
a common API that can

227
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:30,385
be shared across

228
00:09:30,385 --> 00:09:33,550
all these different Unix
and Linux variance.

229
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:36,400
The Open Software Foundation

230
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:40,000
created what was called the
Single Unix Specification.

231
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,545
Originally the IEEE POSIX
specification cost money.

232
00:09:44,545 --> 00:09:47,665
But the Single Unix
Specification was free and

233
00:09:47,665 --> 00:09:51,730
it ultimately incorporated
the entire POSIX standard.

234
00:09:51,730 --> 00:09:56,770
This was a big part of why
POSIX was ultimately adopted,

235
00:09:56,770 --> 00:10:00,940
because it became part of the
Single Unix Specification.

236
00:10:00,940 --> 00:10:04,330
POSIX is given define the
C code API for many of

237
00:10:04,330 --> 00:10:05,350
the concepts that we're going to

238
00:10:05,350 --> 00:10:07,390
cover throughout this semester.

239
00:10:07,390 --> 00:10:09,175
File and directory operation,

240
00:10:09,175 --> 00:10:11,185
clocks and timers, semaphores,

241
00:10:11,185 --> 00:10:12,865
shared memory, threads.

242
00:10:12,865 --> 00:10:15,610
A large amount of what we'll
be doing from users pace is

243
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:18,955
going to be defined by
the POSIX API standard.

244
00:10:18,955 --> 00:10:20,935
There's an official list of

245
00:10:20,935 --> 00:10:22,720
headers you could
find at this link.

246
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:24,175
There's lots of headers that you

247
00:10:24,175 --> 00:10:26,230
probably are familiar with
and you have used already.

248
00:10:26,230 --> 00:10:28,790
For instance, standard
IO, standardint.h.

249
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:30,400
Those are examples of

250
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:33,190
files that are covered
in the POSIX standards.

251
00:10:33,190 --> 00:10:34,780
You've probably been
using this already.

252
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:38,210
You might not have realized
that you were using POSIX.